      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224440",
      ":question/problem": "A light bulb factory's report estimates that $2%$ of the light bulbs produced by its machines are defective. However, a supervisor estimates that this percentage is larger. He conducts a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to test his claim.\n\nAfter randomly sampling $50$ light bulbs, the supervisor notices that $5$ of them are defective. The supervisor compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X\u223cB(50, 0.02)$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.7358$ | $0.9216$ | $0.9822$ | $0.9968$ | $0.9995$ | $0.9999$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of randomly selected light bulbs that are defective, then an appropriate test statistic is\n$X = 5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the percentage of defective light bulbs is larger than reported."
